WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.279
Secure Go handlers should be small enough
to review and predictable enough to test.

2
00:00:04.359 --> 00:00:07.759
A common shape is straightforward: check
the method or route assumptions,

3
00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:09.836
limit and parse the request, validate
data,

4
00:00:09.916 --> 00:00:13.479
read the authentication context, authorize
the action, call business logic,

5
00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:16.420
persist changes where appropriate, and
write a safe response.

6
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.443
This does not require heavy architecture.

7
00:00:18.523 --> 00:00:20.682
It means the trust decisions should be
visible.

8
00:00:20.762 --> 00:00:23.407
When parsing, validation, authorization,
persistence,

9
00:00:23.487 --> 00:00:25.485
and response writing are tangled together,

10
00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:28.371
reviewers have to guess which assumptions
protect the route.

11
00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:31.042
Clear boundaries make it easier to test
malformed input,

12
00:00:31.122 --> 00:00:35.171
unauthorized access, missing records,
downstream failures, and successful behavior.

13
00:00:35.251 --> 00:00:36.978
Middleware can support consistency.

14
00:00:37.058 --> 00:00:40.027
Request IDs, logging context,
authentication, panic recovery,

15
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:43.454
common headers, timeouts, and basic
request shaping are good middleware

16
00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:44.451
responsibilities.

17
00:00:44.531 --> 00:00:48.364
Middleware should be ordered intentionally
and documented so developers know what

18
00:00:48.444 --> 00:00:49.524
each layer guarantees.

19
00:00:49.604 --> 00:00:53.490
It should not hide sensitive authorization
decisions so deeply that route reviewers

20
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:54.380
cannot find them.

21
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.267
Context propagation matters for API
reliability and safety.

22
00:00:57.347 --> 00:01:00.909
Handlers should pass request context to
database calls, downstream clients,

23
00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:04.768
queues, and background work that should
stop when the request is cancelled or times

24
00:01:04.848 --> 00:01:05.064
out.

25
00:01:05.144 --> 00:01:08.814
A lost context can turn a cancelled
request into continued work that nobody owns.

26
00:01:08.894 --> 00:01:10.892
Response helpers can improve consistency.

27
00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:14.696
Status codes, validation errors, not-found
behavior, authorization failures,

28
00:01:14.776 --> 00:01:17.259
and internal failures should be mapped
deliberately.

29
00:01:17.339 --> 00:01:19.444
The safer handler is not the fanciest
handler.

30
00:01:19.524 --> 00:01:21.575
It is the one whose order,
responsibilities,

31
00:01:21.655 --> 00:01:23.545
and failure behavior are easy to explain.

32
00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.514
Secure structure also helps onboarding.

33
00:01:25.594 --> 00:01:29.265
New maintainers can follow an existing
route pattern and understand where to add

34
00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:30.532
checks without guessing.

35
00:01:30.612 --> 00:01:32.555
When the shape of a handler is
predictable,

36
00:01:32.635 --> 00:01:36.522
code review can focus on the specific
business decision instead of rediscovering the

37
00:01:36.602 --> 00:01:38.059
framework behavior every time.

38
00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:41.540
That consistency is a quiet but powerful
control for long-lived services.
